<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Castel Sant'Angelo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd century</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Mausoleum of Hadrian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Castel Sant'Angelo is a fortress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a>, built in the second century as the mausoleum of the emperor Hadrian. Converted into a fortress in late antiquity, it was linked to the Vatican by a fortified corridor and served the popes as a citadel, prison and refuge, notably for Clement VII during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527">Sack of Rome</a> in 1527. It is now a museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/castel-santangelo.jpg" alt="Castel Sant'Angelo" width="320" height="287" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Livioandronico2013 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castel_Sant%27Angelo_at_Night.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The tomb of the Roman emperor Hadrian was erected on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> between 134 and 139. Originally it was a decorated cylinder with a garden on top and a golden quadriga. Hadrian's ashes were placed there a year after his death at Baiae in 138, together with those of his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabina">Sabina</a> and of his first adopted son, Lucius Aelius, who died in 138. The remains of later emperors were also deposited there, the last recorded being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracalla">Caracalla</a> in 217. Hadrian also built the Pons Aelius, which faces the mausoleum and still gives an approach from the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The building became a military fortress in 401 and was included in the Aurelian Walls under Flavius Honorius Augustus. Visigoth looters scattered the urns and ashes during Alaric's sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 410. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> besieged Rome in 537, the defenders threw the original bronze and stone statuary down upon them, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> recounts. Stone from the tomb was later reused: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giorgio-vasari">Giorgio Vasari</a> writes that columns were taken from it for Saint Peter's. The capstone of a funerary urn, probably Hadrian's, ended up in Saint Peter's Basilica, covered the tomb of Otto II and was later built into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> baptistery.</p><p>The popes turned the structure into a castle, beginning in the 14th century, and linked it to Saint Peter's Basilica by a covered fortified corridor, the Passetto di Borgo. Pope Clement VII took refuge there from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landsknecht">Landsknechte</a> of Charles V during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527">Sack of Rome</a> in 1527. Leo X built a chapel, and Paul III added a rich apartment so that a pope would have a fitting place to stay in any later siege. The Papal State also used it as a prison, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giordano-bruno">Giordano Bruno</a>, held there for six years, and Benvenuto Cellini were among its prisoners.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>According to legend, the Archangel Michael appeared on top of the mausoleum and sheathed his sword to mark the end of the plague of 590, which gave the castle its present name. In 1536 Raffaello da Montelupo made a marble statue of Saint Michael to stand on the roof. Peter Anton von Verschaffelt's bronze statue of the same subject replaced it in 1753, and Montelupo's can be seen in a courtyard inside. The castle is the setting for the third act of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giacomo-puccini">Giacomo Puccini</a>'s opera Tosca of 1900. It was decommissioned in 1901 and is now the Museo Nazionale di Castel Sant'Angelo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Castel Sant'Angelo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1797 a gunpowder explosion in the <b>Castel Sant'Angelo</b> damaged part of the Flood fresco and one of the ignudi, which a drawing by a pupil of Michelangelo preserved.</blockquote><a href="/work/sistine-chapel-ceiling">Sistine Chapel ceiling · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Buried first at Puteoli, his remains were later moved to Rome, and his ashes were placed with those of his wife Vibia Sabina in the <b>Mausoleum of Hadrian</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-10">10 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Unpaid troops of Emperor Charles V stormed Rome, killing thousands and looting churches while Pope Clement VII sheltered in <b>Castel Sant'Angelo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-6">6 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
